WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:13.360
There are over 325,000 models on Huggingface and&nbsp;
thousands more are being added. And why might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:21.000
you choose to use AI models like these? Well,&nbsp;
let's start by getting a few things straight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.200
The models we're talking about in this video,&nbsp;
they're specifically LLMs, and that's large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.000
language models, which are foundation models&nbsp;
that use artificial intelligence, deep learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.960
and massive datasets to generate text. We're&nbsp;
talking generative AI. And there are two types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:49.400
generative AI models: There's proprietary models,&nbsp;
and there are open source models. Now, proprietary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:56.720
LLMs, those are owned by a company who can control&nbsp;
its usage. A proprietary LLM may include a license&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.320
that restricts how the LLM can be used. On&nbsp;
the other hand, open source LLMs are free and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.200
available for anyone to access, and developers and&nbsp;
researchers are free to use, improve or otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:16.240
modify the model. Now look, it's not true in every&nbsp;
instance, but generally many proprietary LLMs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:26.200
far larger in size than open source models. And&nbsp;
specifically in terms of parameter size. Some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:35.240
the leading proprietary LLMs extend to thousands&nbsp;
of billions of parameters. Probably? Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:41.800
we don't necessarily know because, well, those&nbsp;
LLMs and that parameter counts are proprietary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:48.560
But bigger isn't necessarily better. And the&nbsp;
open source model ecosystem is showing promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:58.720
in challenging the proprietary LLM business model.&nbsp;
So let's discuss the benefits of open source LLMs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.760
Let's talk about the types of organizations&nbsp;
that are using them. Let's talk about some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:12.160
the leading open source models available today,&nbsp;
and we should talk about the risks associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:20.080
with using them. Now, clearly, one of the&nbsp;
benefits of a open source large language model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:27.680
that has to be transparency. Open source LLMs&nbsp;
may offer better insight into how they work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:35.208
their architecture, and the training data used to&nbsp;
develop them. Another big one is pre-trained open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.208 --> 00:02:43.040
source LLMs allow a process called fine tuning.&nbsp;
That means you can add features to the LLM that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:50.240
benefit your specific use case and the LLMs&nbsp;
can be trained on specific data sets. So I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:03:00.200
fine tune an LLM with my own data. And community&nbsp;
contributions are a big plus. Using a proprietary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:06.040
LLM means you're reliant on a single provider,&nbsp;
whereas open source models benefit from community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:12.280
contributions and multiple service providers. You&nbsp;
can experiment and use contributions from people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:19.320
with varying perspectives. And these benefits&nbsp;
have led to all sorts of organizations to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:27.880
open source LLMs. In another video, I addressed&nbsp;
how NASA and IBM developed an open source LLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:35.560
trained on geospatial data. Some healthcare&nbsp;
organizations use open source LLMs for diagnostic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:46.320
tools and treatment optimization. There's an open&nbsp;
source LLM called FinGPT [fin / financial]. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.760
developed for the financial industry. Which brings&nbsp;
us onto the topic of talking about some specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.760 --> 00:04:01.600
open source LLMs that you might find of interest.&nbsp;
Now Huggingface maintains an open LLM leaderboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:11.080
and that tracks , ranks, and evaluates open&nbsp;
source LLMs on various benchmarks like which LLM&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:19.440
is scoring highest on the Truthful AI Benchmark&nbsp;
series, which measures whether a language model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:28.960
is truthful in generating answers to questions. So&nbsp;
it gives those answers a score. And the top spots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:34.360
on this leaderboard, they change frequently.&nbsp;
And it's quite fun to watch the progress these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:42.240
models are making. Many of the models on the&nbsp;
leaderboard are variations on the Llama 2 open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:48.560
source LLM. That's the one provided by Meta&nbsp;
AI. And Llama 2 encompasses pre-trained and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:56.320
fine tuned generative text models from 70 billion&nbsp;
all the way down to 7 billion parameters. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:04.560
licensed for commercial use. Vicuna was created&nbsp;
on top of the Llama model and fine tuned to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.680
instructions. And then it's also Bloom by BigScience, which is a multilingual language model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:19.280
created by more than 1000 AI researchers. Now, one&nbsp;
area that both proprietary and open source LLMs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:27.320
share is their associated risks. Although LLM&nbsp;
outputs often sounds fluent and authoritative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:36.440
they can be confidently wrong. Hallucinations,&nbsp;
they can result from the LLM being trained on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:44.800
incomplete, contradictory, or inaccurate data&nbsp;
from misunderstanding context. Bias happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:51.840
when the source of data is not diverse or not&nbsp;
representative. And security problems can include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:59.040
leaking PII, and cybercriminals using the LLMs for&nbsp;
malicious tasks like phishing. Especially in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:07.200
early days of large language models, we do need to&nbsp;
mitigate risk. But open source LLMs are thriving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:15.120
in business. Here at IBM, the Watsonx.ai Studio&nbsp;
makes available access to multiple Llama 2 models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.200
and IBM has released a series of foundation&nbsp;
models of its own called Granite. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:29.400
space is changing rapidly, making open source&nbsp;
LLMs a field well-worth keeping a close eye on.

